I don't think this is an important difference.  In chess, you have to 
make strategical decisions, too, and there are programs playing chess on 
a par with grand masters.  They are better at tactical decisions, true, 
but they manage quite well even in closed positions, where strategy 
dominates.

In my opinion, nethack would have to be played by an expert system 
rather than a neural network or a genetic algorithm.  And I don't think 
that it would be impossible to program a successful system, if you put 
in as much work as has gone into the development of chess programs.

By the way, if we take the ascension ratio as a measure for the quality 
of a playing bot and compare it with the best of human players, what do 
people think would be achievable, in principle?  70%, 80% or more like 
50%?  Averaged over all classes?
